<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alfonso VII of León and Castile</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1105–<a href="/years/1157">1157</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Alfonso VII of Castile, Alfonso the Emperor</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alfonso VII was King of León and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> from 1126 and was crowned Emperor of All <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1135. He exploited the collapse of Almoravid power to intervene in the second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifas</a>, made Muslim rulers his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> and in 1147 captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almería</a> with Genoese and Catalan help. Almería was lost to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a> in 1157, the year he died.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alfonso-vii-of-leon-and-castile.jpg" alt="Alfonso VII of León and Castile" width="205" height="376" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous; derivative work by Agricolae · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alfonso-Privilegium.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Alfonso VII</b> recovered the offensive, campaigned into Andalusia and took Almería in 1147 with Genoese and Catalan help, though it was lost in 1157.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-castile">Kingdom of Castile · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The kings stressed their descent from the Visigoths, and from the tenth century some styled themselves emperor (imperator), a claim to precedence over the other Christian rulers of Iberia: Alfonso VI called himself emperor of all Spain, and <b>Alfonso VII</b> was crowned emperor in León in 1135.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-leon">Kingdom of León · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Alfonso VII of León</b> accepted the royal title at the Treaty of Zamora in 1143, and Pope Alexander III recognised Afonso I as king in the bull Manifestis probatum of 1179.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The young commune also fought for the western Mediterranean, joining <b>Alfonso VII of Castile</b> against Almería and Ramon Berenguer IV of Barcelona against Tortosa in 1146–1148.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Republic of Genoa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Treaty of Zamora, <b>Alfonso VII of León and Castile</b> acknowledged his cousin Afonso Henriques as king of Portugal, in the presence of the papal legate.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-5">5 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1147 <b>Alfonso VII of Castile</b>, styled Emperor of Spain, led a coalition including Genoa, Pisa, Ramon Berenguer IV of Barcelona and García Ramírez of Navarre against Almería, then a corsair base.</blockquote><a href="/state/castilian-almeria">Castilian Almería</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Caldas de Reis, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Despeñaperros, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.6,-8.64&amp;year=1138" aria-label="Caldas de Reis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4341 649 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Alfonso VII of León and Castile"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4791 919h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4791 919h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.6,-8.64&amp;year=1138">What was here in 1138? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
